\subsection{Q2, model complexity: when does transfer help?}
\label{sec:q2}

Whether the source helps at all is judged against the baseline, each regressor fit
on the target data alone. On the aligned lumped target (TBCM, the same family as
the BCM source), the source is already a strong prior, by itself reaching an $F_0$
$R^2$ of @@TBCM_SOURCE_F0@@ with no target data, and optimized transfer clears the
baseline, raising the $F_0$ $R^2$ from @@TBCM_BASE_F0@@ to @@TBCM_TRANS_F0@@ at
$N=50$. On the far continuum target (BM), the same source is less accurate than
guessing each output's average value, and transfer does not clear the baseline at
all, an $F_0$ $R^2$ of @@BM_BASE_F0_3@@ against @@BM_TRANS_F0_3@@: the source is
mostly dead weight. Transfer's payoff therefore tracks how close the source is to
the target, exactly the principle it rests on. TabPFN, which uses no source, is
the strongest method on the far target and stays close to the best on the aligned
one, with an $F_0$ $R^2$ of @@TBCM_TABPFN_F0@@ on TBCM and @@BM_TABPFN_F0@@ on BM
at $N=50$, and so removes the dependence on a well-aligned source altogether.

\input{table2_fidelity.tex}

Beyond point accuracy, a useful surrogate must reconstruct the physiological
structure of the $(a_{CT} \times a_{TA})$ control space. On TBCM, which carries a
dense ODE ground-truth map, TabPFN reconstructs the full motor-control surface
from as few as @@TBCM_MAP_N@@ samples and converges to the ground truth by
$N=1000$, with lower image-level error than optimized transfer from $N=50$ upward
(Fig.~\ref{fig:q2}). The same behavior holds on BM, where against a dense
full-data reference TabPFN resolves the correct surface by
$N \approx @@BM_MAP_N@@$ while the transfer surface stays blocky and dragged
toward the misaligned source.

